import type { ResearchPost } from './fleet-data';

export const novemberEighteenResearchBatch: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"developeroffshore-defect-escape-rate-in-outsourced-application-maintenance","title":"Defect escape rate in outsourced application maintenance","excerpt":"An analysis of defect escape rate in outsourced maintenance: what counts as an escape, the denominator, the discovery window, severity weighting, attribution, and a practical protocol.","published":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines defect escape rate as a maintenance signal in outsourced application work. The unit is one defect that reached a later stage than the team intended, usually production or a customer-facing acceptance step. The scope covers defects found after a change was believed complete, including those found by users, monitoring, or a later test cycle. It does not treat every production issue as an escape, because some issues come from environment changes or third-party failures. The analysis uses published quality guidance rather than a proprietary benchmark.","The question is whether escape rate can help a buyer judge the health of a maintenance arrangement without turning it into a vendor score. Escapes are costly and visible, but they are also easy to count in ways that mislead. The report separates the count from the cause and shows where the denominator and the discovery window change the meaning. A rate without that context is a prompt for a conversation, not a conclusion."]},{"heading":"What counts as an escape","body":["Start with the stage where the defect should have been caught. A defect that passes code review and unit tests but fails integration is an escape from the first two stages. A defect that passes all pre-release checks and reaches a user is an escape from the release process. The stage matters because it points to the control that failed.","Exclude issues that were never in scope for the team, such as a vendor outage or a change made outside the reviewed pipeline. Include them in a separate category so they are visible without distorting the rate. Write the definition down and version it. Two teams that define escape differently will compare numbers that mean different things."]},{"heading":"Denominator and discovery window","body":["The denominator can be completed changes, released changes, or production incidents. Each answers a different question. A rate against released changes shows how often a release carries an escape, while a rate against completed work shows how often finished work fails later. Report the denominator with the rate, not after it.","The discovery window changes the count. A thirty-day window captures immediate failures, while a ninety-day window captures slower defects. Neither is wrong, but the choice must be stable across periods. Record the window and the date the data was pulled. A rate that grows only because the window changed is not a trend."]},{"heading":"Severity weighting","body":["A count treats a broken label and a data-loss defect as equal. Weighting by severity gives a more useful view, but it also introduces judgment. Use a small scale tied to user impact and data risk, and keep the raw counts beside the weighted score. The reader should be able to see both.","Avoid a weighting formula that only the metrics owner understands. A simple rule such as minor equals one, major equals five, and critical equals twenty is enough to start. Review the weights with the buyer and keep them stable for at least two quarters. Changing weights whenever the score looks bad destroys trust in the measure."]},{"heading":"Attribution across the handoff","body":["An escape can originate in the brief, the build, the review, the test data, or the release. Assigning the cause to the pod by default hides the part the buyer played. Record the stage where the defect was introduced and the stage where it should have been caught. Those are often different.","Use a short cause list: unclear requirement, missed context, implementation error, review gap, test gap, and environment difference. Add a free-text note for the specific evidence. Review a sample of causes with both sides before reporting the trend. Attribution is a hypothesis until someone checks it.","A severity mix can change for reasons unrelated to quality. A new customer segment may report more minor issues, or a monitoring change may surface defects that were always present. Compare the mix with the release volume and the reporting channel before drawing a conclusion. If the mix shifts sharply, inspect a sample of the new reports. The raw count may be stable while the experience of users has changed."]},{"heading":"Measurement without blame","body":["An escape rate tied to individual consequences will be managed. People will avoid reporting, relabel defects, or delay a release to move the discovery into a different window. The control is aggregate reporting and a clear statement that the purpose is learning. A team that can discuss an escape without fear will surface more of them.","Pair the rate with review depth and test coverage signals. A low escape rate with almost no review may mean the review is shallow, not that the work is clean. A higher rate with strong review may mean the team is catching issues closer to the source. Neither number stands alone."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Define the stage, the severity scale, and the discovery window. Collect the data for one quarter before comparing anything. Segment by work class and change size, because a small configuration change and a large migration have different risk. Report the raw count, the rate, and the severity mix together.","Pick one cause from the sample and fix it. If unclear requirements dominate, improve the intake. If review gaps dominate, adjust the reviewer or the checklist. Measure the same fields for a month before judging the fix. The protocol is working when the cause mix changes, not only when the rate falls.","A useful review separates the defect from the discovery channel. A defect found by a customer and one found by an internal check can have the same severity but a different cost. Record who found it and how long it took. That detail shows whether the controls are catching problems early or whether users are doing the detection. It also prevents a drop in customer reports from being read as an improvement when the product simply stopped being used."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A pod releases forty changes in a quarter and five escapes are reported. The blended rate is twelve and a half percent. The breakdown shows three escapes in one integration area that changed vendor twice, and two minor defects in unrelated screens. The headline number suggests a broad problem that the detail does not support.","The team treats the integration area separately and adds a contract test plus a named reviewer. The minor defects get a small checklist item. The next quarter the rate falls, but the more useful result is that the largest cause now has an owner. The number followed the fix rather than leading it.","Keep the report to one page and attach the sample behind it. The page should state the definition, the window, the rate, and the one cause the team is addressing. A long report invites debate about the method instead of the fix. Review the page with the buyer and the pod together so both sides hear the same evidence. The next review can then compare like with like."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the defect, the stage introduced, the stage detected, the severity, the release date, and the discovery date. Add the work class, the change size, and whether the defect was found by a user, monitoring, or an internal check. Use controlled values for the cause so the data can be aggregated. Keep the raw data available so a reviewer can recheck the sample without rebuilding the report.","Collect for at least a quarter before drawing a trend. Check a sample against the tickets and the release records to confirm the stage assignment. If two reviewers disagree, write a short rule and apply it again. The quality of the rate depends on consistent classification more than on the size of the dataset."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Some escapes are healthy because they come from fast feedback in a complex system. A team that releases often will find some defects in production before a slower team would find them in a long test cycle. The rate should be read with release frequency and change size. Label the definition version beside the chart so a later reader knows which rules produced the number.","Escape data also depends on honest reporting and stable tooling. A change in the incident system can make the trend incomparable. Record the tool and the definition version with the numbers. Treat the rate as a conversation aid, not as a contractual verdict without context."]}],"sources":[{"name":"ISTQB glossary, escaped defect","url":"https://glossary.istqb.org/en_US/term/escaped-defect"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final"},{"name":"CISA Secure by Design","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design"}],"keyStats":["3 stages to record: where the defect was introduced, where it should have been caught, and where it was found","2 denominators to report: released changes and completed work items","1 stable discovery window, recorded with the pull date","4 cause groups: requirement, context, implementation, and verification"],"takeaways":["Define the escape stage and the discovery window before counting anything, because both change the rate.","Report the raw count, the rate, and the severity mix together instead of one blended score.","Attribute the defect to the stage where it was introduced and the stage that should have caught it.","Pair the rate with review depth and test evidence so a low number does not reward shallow checks.","Fix one cause at a time and measure the same fields before judging the result."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is a lower escape rate always better?","answer":"No. A very low rate can mean the definition is narrow or the later checks are shallow. Read it beside review depth, test evidence, and release frequency."},{"question":"Should buyer-caused issues count as escapes?","answer":"Count them in a separate cause category so they are visible without being attributed to the pod. The point is to see where the gap originated."},{"question":"How long should the discovery window be?","answer":"Use one stable window, often thirty or ninety days, and record it with the data. The right length depends on how quickly the product and its users reveal defects."}],"related":[{"title":"Review batch size as a delivery control in offshore engineering","href":"/research/developeroffshore-review-batch-size-as-a-delivery-control-in-offshore-engineering"},{"title":"Write a bug reproduction packet for offshore support","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-write-a-bug-reproduction-packet-for-offshore-support"},{"title":"Rework rate as a delivery signal in outsourced development","href":"/research/developeroffshore-rework-rate-as-a-delivery-signal-in-outsourced-development"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-test-flakiness-cost-in-offshore-qa-automation","title":"Test flakiness cost in offshore QA automation","excerpt":"An analysis of flaky-test cost in offshore QA: the definition, the attention cost, detection signals, quarantine rules, fix versus delete, ownership, and a measured protocol.","published":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines the cost of flaky tests in offshore QA automation. The unit is one automated check that produces different results on the same code without a relevant change. The scope covers UI, API, and integration checks that run in a shared pipeline. It does not cover tests that fail because the product changed, which are evidence rather than flakiness.","The question is how a buyer can see the cost of flakiness and decide what to fix, quarantine, or delete. Flaky tests are often treated as an annoyance, yet they consume review time and teach teams to ignore red builds. The report treats flakiness as a reliability and attention problem, not only a tooling problem. The report is about the reliability of the signal, not about the diligence of the tester."]},{"heading":"What flakiness includes","body":["A test is flaky when the same revision passes and fails without a code change. Common causes include timing, shared data, order dependence, network variance, and environment drift. A test that fails because the product is genuinely broken is not flaky. The distinction matters because the remedies are different. The threshold belongs in the suite documentation so everyone applies the same rule. It should be reviewed when the pipeline changes.","Some teams count a test as flaky after one retry, while others require several observations. Define the threshold and record the evidence. A single failure may be an infrastructure event, while a repeated pattern is a property of the test. Use the stricter definition for quarantine decisions."]},{"heading":"The cost model","body":["The visible cost is the time spent rerunning a failed job. The larger cost is the attention spent deciding whether a red result is real. Each false failure interrupts a reviewer and erodes trust in the suite. Over a month, that attention can exceed the time needed to fix the test.","Add the cost of delayed merges and the risk of a real failure being ignored. A team that reruns by habit may eventually miss a genuine defect. The cost model should include rerun minutes, reviewer interruptions, and the number of real failures found after a retry. Those three numbers are usually enough to justify repair work."]},{"heading":"Detection signals","body":["Track pass and fail rates by test and by revision. A test that fails intermittently on unchanged code is the clearest signal. Also watch tests that pass only after a retry and tests that fail in one environment but not another. Group the signals by suite and by cause.","Keep the run history long enough to see a pattern. A single week may not distinguish a flaky test from a temporary service issue. A four-week window with revision identifiers is a practical starting point. Review the top offenders rather than every test.","A team can estimate the cost without perfect data. Count the reruns for one week and multiply by the average wait for a pipeline slot. Add the number of review comments that only ask for a rerun. That rough figure is enough to rank the problem against other work. Precision can come later, after the team agrees the cost is real."]},{"heading":"Quarantine rules","body":["A quarantine removes a flaky test from the blocking set while keeping it visible. The rule should state who can quarantine, for how long, and what evidence is required. A quarantine without an owner becomes a deletion with extra steps. Put the test in a named list and review the list on a schedule.","Quarantine should not hide the risk the test was covering. Note the user journey or contract behind the check and add a manual or lower-level check if needed. If the test covers a critical path, the quarantine needs a named approver. The goal is to protect the pipeline without pretending the risk disappeared."]},{"heading":"Fix versus delete","body":["Fix the test when it covers a behavior that still matters and the cause is removable. Improve the wait condition, isolate the data, or remove the order dependency. Delete it when the behavior is no longer relevant or when the maintenance cost exceeds the value. Both decisions are valid when they are recorded.","Avoid fixing a test by adding a longer sleep. A sleep hides the timing problem and makes the suite slower. Prefer a condition that waits for the actual state. If the product has no observable state to wait for, that is a design finding worth raising.","The quarantine list should be short enough to read in a stand-up. Each entry needs the test, the owner, the reason, and the expiry date. A long list is a sign that the suite is being maintained by accumulation rather than design. Review the list with the product owner when a critical path is involved. The conversation is usually more valuable than the tooling."]},{"heading":"Ownership and cadence","body":["Give the suite an owner, not a committee. The owner reviews the flaky list, assigns repairs, and removes tests that no longer earn their place. The owner can be a QA lead in the pod with a named buyer counterpart for critical paths. Review the list weekly and the trend monthly.","Make flakiness visible in the same place as other quality signals. A short section in the delivery review is enough. Do not create a separate ceremony that competes with the work. The owner should bring one cause, one fix, and one measured result.","A fix should include a check that would have caught the flake. If the cause was shared data, add a test that runs in parallel with the same fixture. If the cause was timing, add a state assertion that fails when the wait is too short. The new check protects the fix from being undone by a later change. Record the cause in the commit message so the next engineer understands the choice."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Collect four weeks of run history with test identifiers and revision hashes. Identify the tests that fail on unchanged code or pass only after a retry. Rank them by rerun minutes and reviewer interruptions. Pick the top three and assign a cause.","Fix one, quarantine one, and delete one if the evidence supports it. Measure the same signals for a month. Compare the failure rate and the rerun time before and after. The protocol is working when the suite becomes more trustworthy, not only faster.","The owner should publish the list where the team can see it, not in a private document. A shared view lets a reviewer check whether a red check is already known to be flaky. It also makes the repair work visible when the sprint is full. Visibility is not the same as priority, but it is a start. The owner can then bring the top item to the delivery review with evidence."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A suite of two hundred checks fails about fifteen times a week. Eight failures come from three tests that wait on a shared account. Each failure triggers a rerun and a review comment. The team estimates two hours a week lost to reruns and another two hours lost to triage.","The team isolates the account data, replaces one fixed sleep with a state check, and deletes a test for a removed screen. The weekly failures fall to four. The remaining two are quarantined with an owner and a review date. The suite is not perfect, but the team trusts it more."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Not every intermittent failure is a test problem. A service that is genuinely unstable will produce variable results, and fixing the test would hide a real issue. Check the product and environment before assuming the test is at fault. The distinction changes the remedy.","A zero-flake target can also be counterproductive if it encourages teams to delete useful checks. The goal is a suite whose failures mean something. Accept some residual noise and focus on the tests that consume the most attention. A suite that fails loudly but rarely is more useful than one that fails often and means nothing."]}],"sources":[{"name":"Google Testing Blog, Flaky Tests at Google and How We Mitigate Them","url":"https://testing.googleblog.com/2016/05/flaky-tests-at-google-and-how-we.html"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final"},{"name":"CISA Secure by Design","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design"}],"keyStats":["2 cost types: rerun minutes and reviewer interruptions","3 responses: fix, quarantine, or delete, each with an owner and a date","4 detection signals: unchanged-code failure, retry-only pass, environment variance, and order dependence","1 rule: a quarantined test keeps its risk visible and has a review date"],"takeaways":["Define flakiness by the same-revision result, not by any red build that needs a rerun.","Put attention cost beside rerun time, because repeated false failures erode trust in the suite.","Quarantine with an owner, an expiry date, and a note about the risk the test covered.","Prefer a state-based wait over a longer sleep when fixing a timing failure.","Measure the top offenders and one fix at a time instead of trying to eliminate all noise."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should a flaky test block a release?","answer":"Usually not while it is quarantined, but the risk it covered must be visible. A critical-path test needs a named approver and a replacement check."},{"question":"How long can a test stay quarantined?","answer":"Set a short expiry, such as one or two sprints, and review it on a schedule. A quarantine without a date becomes a permanent gap."},{"question":"Is retrying a failed test acceptable?","answer":"A single retry can separate a transient infrastructure event from a repeatable failure. Repeated retries without investigation hide the problem and consume time."}],"related":[{"title":"Automated test evidence for distributed development teams","href":"/research/developeroffshore-automated-test-evidence-for-distributed-development-teams"},{"title":"Set a review turnaround target for an offshore pod","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-set-a-review-turnaround-target-for-an-offshore-pod"},{"title":"Queue wait time as a delivery constraint in offshore code review","href":"/research/developeroffshore-queue-wait-time-as-a-delivery-constraint-in-offshore-code-review"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-change-lead-time-decomposition-in-outsourced-delivery","title":"Change lead time decomposition in outsourced delivery","excerpt":"An analysis of change lead time in outsourced delivery: the start and end, the four segments, wait versus work, handoff effects, measurement pitfalls, and a practical protocol.","published":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines change lead time as a delivery signal in outsourced software work. The unit is one change from the moment it is ready to be worked on to the moment it is running in production. The scope covers the waiting and working time inside that path, including review, testing, approval, and release. It excludes discovery work that precedes a defined change.","The question is whether a buyer can use lead time to find coordination cost without turning it into a speed contest. A short lead time is not automatically good if it skips review, and a long one is not automatically bad if the change is risky. The report decomposes the time so the team can see where it waits and why. The decomposition is a diagnostic, not a target that every change must meet."]},{"heading":"Define the start and end","body":["Choose a start that the team controls. Ready for development is more useful than the date a customer asked, because it separates discovery from delivery. Choose an end that reflects real use, such as deployed and serving traffic. A merge is not the end if the change waits for a release train.","Record the definition and keep it stable. A team that moves the start earlier can appear faster without changing anything real. A team that moves the end to merge can hide the release queue. Write both boundaries in the metric definition and review them when the process changes."]},{"heading":"The four segments","body":["Split the time into queue, active work, review, and release. Queue is the wait before someone starts. Active work is the time spent building and testing. Review is the wait for a reviewer and the response. Release is the wait for approval, a window, or a deploy.","Report each segment separately and as a share of the total. A total of ten days may be two days of work and eight days of waiting. The share tells the team where to look. The absolute number tells them how much room there is to improve.","The segments should add up to the total, with a small allowance for clock gaps. If they do not, the timestamps are inconsistent or a step is missing. Reconcile the difference before reporting a trend. A decomposition that does not sum is a sign that the process has a step nobody has named."]},{"heading":"Wait time versus work time","body":["Wait time is often the largest segment and the least visible. A ticket can sit in a queue because the pod is at capacity, because a dependency is missing, or because nobody has decided the priority. Those causes need different responses. A segment that is small in one team may be the largest cost in another, so compare the shape rather than the total.","Distinguish blocked time from idle time. A change waiting for access is blocked, while a change waiting for a free engineer is queued. Record the reason in the ticket when the wait begins. Without the reason, the lead time chart shows a delay but not a decision."]},{"heading":"Handoff and review effects","body":["Every handoff adds a wait. A change that moves from the pod to a buyer reviewer and back can lose a day even when each side responds quickly. The number of handoffs is often a better predictor of lead time than the size of the change. The reason field is more useful than a longer list of statuses because it names the constraint.","Review capacity matters as much as review speed. A reviewer with a full queue will delay every change behind the current one. Track the queue depth and the time to first response, not only the time to approval. Those two signals separate a slow reviewer from an overloaded one. A reason field with no owner is still useful, because it names the constraint for the next review.","A wait reason should be easy to choose from a short list. If the list has twenty options, people will pick the first one or leave it blank. Keep five or six reasons and a note field for the detail. Review the distribution monthly and rename a reason if it is used for everything. The categories should help the team act, not decorate the chart."]},{"heading":"Measurement pitfalls","body":["Averaging lead time hides the shape of the distribution. A few very long changes can raise the mean while most changes are fast. Report the median and the share that exceed a threshold. The tail is where the coordination cost usually lives.","Exclude or separate changes that were deliberately parked. A change waiting for a customer decision is not the same as a change waiting for a free engineer. Tag the reason so the metric reflects the process rather than the calendar. A reviewer who is fast but unavailable for half the day may still be the bottleneck.","Handoff cost can be reduced without removing the review. A clear request with the diff, the risk, and the question lets a reviewer start immediately. A queue with a visible owner and a response target reduces the search time. Those changes do not weaken the control, and they often improve the review because the context arrives with the change."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Instrument the four timestamps: ready, started, review requested, and deployed. Add a reason when a change waits more than one day. Collect for a month before comparing teams or periods. Segment by change size and work class.","Pick the largest wait segment and change one thing. Add a reviewer, clarify a decision right, or reduce the release batch. Measure the same segments for a month. The goal is a shorter path with the same control, not a faster path that skips review.","A dashboard can make a segment look better by changing when a status is set. For example, marking a change as started when it enters a queue removes the queue from the metric. Review the definitions whenever the workflow changes. The number should describe the work, not the label that happens to be available."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A pod reports a median lead time of nine days. The decomposition shows one day of active work, one day of review, and seven days of queue. The queue is not caused by capacity but by a weekly prioritization meeting that assigns work only on Mondays. Use a threshold that fits the work, such as five days for routine changes and a separate view for larger ones.","The team moves small changes to a daily pull queue and keeps the weekly meeting for larger items. The median falls to four days without adding staff. The review segment stays the same, which shows the change did not weaken the control. Keep the reason list short so people actually use it."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the ready date, the start date, the review request date, the approval date, and the deploy date. Add the change size, the work class, and the reason for any wait longer than a day. Use one time source and one time zone. The queue was visible only after the team separated waiting from working.","Collect at least a month before reporting a trend. Check a sample against the ticket history to confirm the timestamps are real. If a timestamp is missing, mark it rather than estimating. A metric built on guesses will not support a decision."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Lead time can reward small, low-risk changes and punish necessary large work. Segment by size so a complex migration is not compared with a copy change. Do not use one target for every class of work. A single timestamp source prevents a disagreement about which clock is correct.","A fast lead time can also mean the team is under-reviewing or releasing without evidence. Pair the metric with change failure rate and review depth. The useful question is whether the path is proportionate to the risk, not whether it is the fastest. Mark estimated values clearly and keep them out of the main trend."]}],"sources":[{"name":"DORA, Lead time for changes","url":"https://dora.dev/devops-capabilities/technical/lead-time-for-changes/"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final"},{"name":"GAO Cost Estimating and Assessment Guide","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-195g"}],"keyStats":["4 segments: queue, active work, review, and release","2 boundary rules: a stable start and an end that reflects real use","3 wait reasons: capacity, dependency, and decision","1 rule: report the median and the tail, not only the average"],"takeaways":["Fix the start and end of the clock before comparing teams or periods.","Decompose the total into queue, active work, review, and release so the largest wait is visible.","Record why a change waited, because capacity, dependency, and decision delays need different fixes.","Report the median and the share above a threshold, because an average hides the slow tail.","Change one segment at a time and pair the result with change failure rate so speed does not replace control."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should lead time start when the customer asks?","answer":"Usually not. Use a start the delivery team controls, such as ready for development, and measure discovery separately."},{"question":"Is a lower lead time always better?","answer":"No. Pair it with change failure rate and review depth. A fast path that skips evidence is not an improvement."},{"question":"How should we handle a deliberately parked change?","answer":"Tag the reason and report it separately. Mixing a customer decision delay with a capacity delay distorts the process signal."}],"related":[{"title":"Queue wait time as a delivery constraint in offshore code review","href":"/research/developeroffshore-queue-wait-time-as-a-delivery-constraint-in-offshore-code-review"},{"title":"Change failure rate as a contract health signal in offshore delivery","href":"/research/developeroffshore-change-failure-rate-as-a-contract-health-signal-in-offshore-delivery"},{"title":"Set a review turnaround target for an offshore pod","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-set-a-review-turnaround-target-for-an-offshore-pod"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-incident-recurrence-rate-in-outsourced-application-support","title":"Incident recurrence rate in outsourced application support","excerpt":"An analysis of incident recurrence in outsourced support: what recurrence means, how to match incidents, the denominator, action quality, blame risk, and a practical protocol.","published":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines incident recurrence as a support signal in outsourced application maintenance. The unit is one incident that shares a root cause with an earlier incident and could have been prevented by the earlier action. The scope covers production incidents and severe support escalations with a recorded cause. It does not count a new incident that merely affects the same service.","The question is whether recurrence can show whether post-incident actions actually changed the system. A team can close actions quickly and still repeat the failure. The report treats recurrence as a test of action quality, not as a count of mistakes. The measure is useful only when the team is willing to link the second incident to the first."]},{"heading":"What recurrence means","body":["Recurrence requires a link between two incidents. The link can be the same root cause, the same failed control, or the same missing monitor. A shared service name is not enough, because many unrelated failures can affect one component. A shared component is a place to look, not a cause by itself.","Some incidents recur because the underlying cause is accepted risk. In that case the recurrence is expected and should be labeled. The useful signal is an unplanned recurrence after an action was marked complete. That gap is where the learning failed."]},{"heading":"Matching incidents","body":["Use a short cause taxonomy and a structured field in the incident record. Match on the cause and the failed control, then review the pair. A text search alone will miss differently worded incidents and create false matches. The link also gives the next responder a starting point, because it turns a label into a short history that can be read in a minute. That history is often enough to choose the next action without reopening the whole review.","Keep a small set of examples for each cause so reviewers apply the taxonomy consistently. Review a sample of matches each quarter. If two reviewers disagree, refine the definition rather than forcing a match. A false match is worse than a missed match because it sends the review toward the wrong action.","A short cause list is easier to apply than a detailed taxonomy. Start with five or six causes that match the team's actual incidents. Add a free-text note for the specific evidence and review the list each quarter. If a new cause appears repeatedly, add it; if one is never used, remove it. The taxonomy should follow the work, not an ideal process."]},{"heading":"The denominator","body":["A raw recurrence count grows with incident volume. Report recurrence as a share of incidents with a recorded cause, and separately for severe incidents. The second view is often more useful because severe incidents receive a deeper review. Severe incidents deserve a separate view because their review depth is usually different.","Exclude incidents with no cause record from the denominator, but report how many were excluded. A high exclusion rate is a data-quality finding. It may mean the review process is skipping the cause step or that the record is hard to complete. A high exclusion rate is a reason to simplify the record, not to pressure responders to guess.","The match should be recorded with a link to the earlier incident, not only a label. A link lets the next reviewer read the original action and its evidence. It also makes the count auditable without a separate spreadsheet. If the link is missing, the match is a guess. Treat the link as part of the definition, not an optional note."]},{"heading":"Action quality","body":["An action can be specific, owned, and testable, or it can be a general instruction to be more careful. Recurrence is more likely after the second kind. Review the action text and ask whether it names a change, an owner, and a check. An action that cannot be checked is a note, not a control.","Separate containment actions from prevention actions. A monitor that detects the failure faster is useful, but it does not prevent recurrence. Record both types and measure them separately. A team can be good at detection and weak at prevention.","A prevention action should name the check that will fail if the cause returns. That check can be a test, a monitor, or a review question. It should be owned by someone who can maintain it. An action without a check is a hope that the team remembers. The review should ask for the check before marking the action complete."]},{"heading":"Attribution and blame","body":["Recurrence is easy to turn into a blame metric. A team that fears blame will avoid linking incidents or will label a repeat as a new cause. The result is a cleaner chart and a worse system. The team should hear that the review is about the system, not about who was on call.","Report recurrence at the service and cause level, not by individual. Discuss the action quality, not the person who wrote it. The goal is to find the gap between a completed action and a changed system. A blame-free review still needs a named owner for each action."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Add a cause and an action-quality field to the incident record. Match new incidents against the last two quarters. Review the matches monthly with the support lead and the buyer's service owner. Report the share and the excluded records.","Pick one recurring cause and inspect the original action. Strengthen it with a test, a monitor, or a code change. Measure recurrence for that cause for a quarter. The protocol is working when the same cause does not return after a completed prevention action.","The review should also protect the responder who reports a repeat. A person who says the same failure has returned is providing useful evidence, not admitting a mistake. Thank the reporter and inspect the action. A team that punishes the messenger will lose the signal and keep the incident."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A service has four incidents in three months, all involving a queue that backs up after a deploy. Each incident has an action to add a dashboard note. The note is added every time, and the queue still backs up. The first review should agree on the cause list, not on a target rate.","The team replaces the note with a deploy check that measures queue depth and a rollback trigger. The next deploy pauses automatically when the depth crosses the threshold. No incident occurs, and the action is now a control rather than a reminder. If the cause returns, reopen the original action rather than writing a second identical one."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the incident date, the service, the cause, the failed control, the action, the action owner, and the completion date. Add whether the action was containment or prevention. Keep the fields short enough that a responder can complete them during the review. Each note looked reasonable in isolation, which is why the pattern was easy to miss.","Collect two quarters if possible, because recurrence needs history. Check a sample of old incidents to confirm the cause field is usable. If the old records are thin, start the taxonomy now and mark the earlier period as incomplete. The new check turned a repeated reminder into a stopping condition."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Some recurrence is acceptable when the cost of prevention exceeds the risk. A rare, low-impact incident may not justify a large engineering change. Record that decision explicitly rather than leaving the action open forever. Two quarters of history is usually enough to see a repeat without waiting a year.","A low recurrence rate can also mean the matching is too narrow or the team is not reporting. Sample the incidents and the links each quarter. The metric is a prompt to inspect action quality, not a proof that the system is safe. An incomplete early period should be labeled rather than silently mixed with the new data. The label should be applied at the time of the match, not reconstructed later."]}],"sources":[{"name":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Computer Security Incident Handling Guide","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final"},{"name":"Google SRE Book, Postmortem Culture","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"name":"CISA Incident and Vulnerability Response Playbooks","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/federal-government-cybersecurity-incident-and-vulnerability-response-playbooks"}],"keyStats":["3 matching fields: root cause, failed control, and service scope","2 action types: containment and prevention","2 rates: recurrence among all incidents and recurrence among severe incidents","1 rule: a completed action is not a prevention action until a check proves it"],"takeaways":["Match incidents on cause and failed control, not on service name alone.","Report recurrence as a share of incidents with a recorded cause and show the excluded records.","Separate containment from prevention, because a faster alert does not stop the failure.","Inspect the original action when a cause returns, and strengthen it with a test or control.","Review recurrence by service and cause, never as an individual score."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is every repeat incident a failure?","answer":"Not always. Some repeats come from accepted risk or a genuinely new cause in the same service. The useful case is an unplanned repeat after a prevention action was marked complete."},{"question":"How far back should matching go?","answer":"Two quarters is a practical start. It covers the life of most post-incident actions without forcing the team to review years of records."},{"question":"Who should own the recurrence review?","answer":"The support lead with the buyer's service owner. Keep the discussion at the service and cause level, not the individual level."}],"related":[{"title":"Post-incident action tracking in distributed engineering teams","href":"/research/developeroffshore-post-incident-action-tracking-in-distributed-engineering-teams"},{"title":"Write a post-incident action tracker for a distributed development pod","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-write-a-post-incident-action-tracker-for-a-distributed-development-pod"},{"title":"On-call load distribution in distributed offshore support","href":"/research/developeroffshore-on-call-load-distribution-in-distributed-offshore-support"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-reviewer-coverage-breadth-in-distributed-code-review","title":"Reviewer coverage breadth in distributed code review","excerpt":"An analysis of reviewer coverage breadth in distributed code review: what breadth means, why concentration forms, how to measure it, quality signals, rotation, and a practical protocol.","published":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines reviewer coverage breadth in distributed code review. The unit is a repository or service area over a period, and the question is how many people can review a change with enough context to approve it. The scope covers technical review, not management sign-off. It focuses on the risk that a small set of reviewers becomes a delivery dependency.","The question is whether breadth can be measured without turning review into a popularity contest. A broad reviewer pool is not automatically better if the reviews are shallow. The report looks for signals that combine coverage with quality, so the buyer can see both the dependency and the depth. The buyer can then see whether the dependency is a staffing issue or a knowledge issue."]},{"heading":"What coverage breadth means","body":["Breadth is the number of qualified reviewers per area, weighted by the share of changes they can handle. A team with five names but one real reviewer has low breadth. A team with two reviewers who each know half the area has partial breadth. A count of repository access rights is not a measure of review coverage.","Breadth includes the ability to review across time zones. A reviewer who is available only during a narrow overlap may not cover the queue in practice. Record the overlap window with the reviewer list. Availability is part of coverage, not a separate concern."]},{"heading":"Why concentration forms","body":["Concentration forms when the same person reviews because they are fast, trusted, or simply online. It also forms when the review tooling routes changes to a default owner. The path of least resistance creates a single point of failure without anyone deciding to. The default routing rule is often invisible until the main reviewer takes a holiday.","Offshore rotations can deepen concentration because context is harder to transfer across shifts. A reviewer who joins later may not feel confident approving without the original owner. That confidence gap is a training and documentation issue, not only a scheduling one. Confidence grows through practice, not through a single walkthrough.","Tooling can create concentration without anyone noticing. A default reviewer, a code owner file, or a routing rule can send most changes to one person. Audit the routing quarterly and ask whether the default still matches the team. A small change to the rule can spread the load without a reorg."]},{"heading":"Measuring breadth","body":["Count the distinct reviewers who approved changes in each area over a quarter. Add the share of changes reviewed by the top reviewer. A high share is a concentration signal even when the total count looks healthy. The matrix should be updated when someone joins, leaves, or changes area. A stale matrix gives false comfort, especially after a rotation. Put the update in the same checklist as the access review so it is not forgotten between quarters.","Segment by change type and risk. A critical migration may legitimately have one specialist reviewer, while routine changes should have broader coverage. Do not apply one threshold to every area. Compare the pattern with the team's own view of who can cover an absence.","A simple matrix of areas and reviewers is enough to start. Mark each reviewer as lead, backup, or learning for each area. The gaps become visible without a complex tool. Review the matrix with the team and ask whether it matches reality. The team will usually name a gap that the approval data missed."]},{"heading":"Review quality signals","body":["Breadth without depth is a rotation of rubber stamps. Pair the coverage measure with review comments, defects found in review, and the time to first response. A new reviewer who catches real issues is adding coverage, while one who approves without reading is not. A review that finds nothing may still be useful, but a pattern of empty approvals deserves a look.","Sample a few reviews from each reviewer and check whether the comments engage with the change. Look for evidence, questions, and specific risks rather than a count of comments. The goal is a qualified pool, not a larger list of names. The sample should include both routine and risky changes.","Review quality is easier to judge from a sample than from a dashboard. Read two or three reviews per reviewer and look for specific comments, questions, and risks. A reviewer who only writes looks good is not adding much. Share the examples in a short session so the standard is visible. The goal is a common bar, not a personal style."]},{"heading":"Rotation and ownership","body":["A rotation can spread review experience, but it needs a stable owner for each area. The owner maintains the checklist and confirms that new reviewers are ready. Rotation without ownership produces turnover without depth. The owner also decides when a reviewer is ready to approve alone.","Pair a new reviewer with an experienced one for a set of changes, then let the new reviewer lead with a backstop. Record the areas where the new reviewer is confident and the areas that still need support. This is more useful than a generic training completion. Write the support areas in the ownership note so the gap is visible."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["List the areas, the qualified reviewers, and the overlap windows. Measure the top reviewer share and the distinct reviewer count for a quarter. Sample the review quality for each reviewer. Identify the areas with one effective reviewer.","Pick one area and add a second reviewer through pairing and a short ownership handoff. Measure the share and the quality again after a quarter. The protocol is working when an absence no longer blocks the queue and the review depth holds. That comparison shows whether the new reviewer added depth or only a name.","A rotation should have an exit condition. A new reviewer becomes covered when they can approve a routine change alone and explain the risk. Record that milestone in the ownership note. Without it, rotation becomes a permanent shadowing arrangement that never transfers authority. The owner should confirm the milestone, not the calendar."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A service has six engineers and two reviewers who approve ninety percent of changes. The other four comment occasionally but rarely approve. The queue stalls when either reviewer is on leave. The top share should fall without the comment quality falling with it.","The lead pairs each of the four with a reviewer for a month and gives them a checklist for routine changes. After a quarter, the top reviewer share falls to sixty percent. Review comments remain stable, and the queue no longer depends on two calendars. If quality drops, add a checklist or a backstop rather than reversing the change."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the reviewer, the area, the change type, the approval date, and the review comments. Add the reviewer's overlap window and whether they were the sole approver. Use a consistent area label so the data can be grouped. The pair worked because the new reviewers had a real checklist and a person to ask.","Collect a quarter of history before drawing a conclusion. Check a sample of approvals against the comments to confirm the reviewer engaged. If the tool does not store comments, use a manual sample. The measure is only as good as the evidence behind it."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Concentration can be appropriate for a small, high-risk area with one genuine specialist. Forcing breadth there could reduce quality. The goal is to identify dependency, not to equalize every count. Sole-approver flags show where the queue depends on one calendar.","Breadth also depends on the size of the team and the number of areas. A small pod will always have fewer reviewers. Compare the pattern with the available people and the risk, and treat the number as one input rather than a target. A manual sample is acceptable when the tooling is thin, as long as the method is recorded. Record the method and the sample size beside the result so the next reviewer can repeat it exactly."]}],"sources":[{"name":"Google Engineering Practices, Code Review","url":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final"},{"name":"CISA Secure by Design","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design"}],"keyStats":["3 coverage measures: distinct approvers, top-reviewer share, and overlap availability","2 quality signals: review comments and defects found in review","3 concentration causes: default routing, trust, and time-zone availability","1 rule: add a second qualified reviewer before an absence blocks the queue"],"takeaways":["Define breadth as qualified reviewers who can approve, not as every name with repository access.","Include the overlap window, because availability is part of real coverage.","Measure the top-reviewer share beside the distinct count so a healthy total cannot hide one bottleneck.","Pair breadth with review comments and defects found, because a rubber stamp is not coverage.","Pair a new reviewer with an owner, then let them lead with a backstop before counting them as covered."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is one specialist reviewer a problem?","answer":"Only if the area needs routine review and an absence blocks the queue. A narrow, high-risk area may justify one specialist with a named backstop."},{"question":"Should every engineer be able to approve every change?","answer":"No. Breadth means enough qualified reviewers for each area, not universal approval rights. Depth and context still matter."},{"question":"How do we know a new reviewer is ready?","answer":"Use a period of paired review, then a lead role with a backstop. Check the comments and the defects found before counting the reviewer as coverage."}],"related":[{"title":"Review batch size as a delivery control in offshore engineering","href":"/research/developeroffshore-review-batch-size-as-a-delivery-control-in-offshore-engineering"},{"title":"Set a code review comment severity scale for offshore work","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-set-a-code-review-comment-severity-scale-for-offshore-work"},{"title":"Knowledge-transfer risk when a developer leaves a distributed team","href":"/research/developeroffshore-knowledge-transfer-risk-when-a-developer-leaves-a-distributed-team"}]}];
